A||||14||Robert||How hard is it to change the water pump||||9||
Z||000000||Robert||01-09-2000||09:51 PM||||My water pump froze up today, the good news is I was only a block from my house.  I have a 87GT with the 2.8.  Is this a job for an average machnic, or should I take it to my local Fiero Shop?<BR>Thanks all<BR>Robert<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||TOM||01-09-2000||09:56 PM||||Hard? They are not hard to do. A little time consuming. If you are a so-so or better mechanic do it yourself. The shops will ream you. You have to jack the car and take the right wheel and well off. Then its pretty straight forward.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||01-09-2000||10:10 PM||||Ditto... Kind of a pain in the butt, but definately do-able. You'll probably need some Torx sockets. Sorry, don't remember what sizes I used. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||01-09-2000||11:13 PM||||The hardest/worst part is cleaning off all the old gasket sealer/permatex.  I've done this three times now on mine, and I finally did it in under 4 hours.  Plan on some raw fingers.  Best thing I got was a can of gasket remover, but it eats your skin.  You'll need the usual suspects (10-15 mm sockets and wrenches, odd extensions, etc, but I don't recall any torx screws on mine (86).  Some of the later years used them (according to the book).  If you've never done this before, match each bolt with its corresponding hole in the new water pump as you remove them, since you'll be working blind on the ones under the pulley.  Be prepared to catch a lot of coolant, even after draining the right side coolant pipe.  I did a flush and refill with the pink stuff (longer life, easier on the pump seals), at the same time.  Best thing is to be patient, since some of the bolts also hold the timing cover on, too, and now might be a good time for a chain and gears, if you have a lot of miles.  It isn't much more work.  Keep us posted.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Robert||01-10-2000||12:23 AM||||Thanks for all your advice.  I'm going to sleep on it tonight and maybe take a stab at it tomorrow.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000005||Fieroguy1||01-10-2000||12:31 AM||||I,replaced mine from the top without removing the wheel in less than 45 mins.||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||01-10-2000||01:15 AM||||That's why I'd never make a living as a professional hourly wage mechanic.  I learned QA from the nuclear Navy.  Clean it, check it, then clean it again anyway.  Better to do it once slowly than to do it twice quickly (if only these replacement pumps had better seals and bearings).  I can do all six plugs in under 30 minutes, though.||||reg||12
Z||000007||terryk||01-10-2000||02:16 AM||||2birds:<P>You just gave the reason for paying the extra money for GM replacement parts.<P>The aftermarket parts are crap except in the rare case. I finally learned to spend the big dollars for parts that won't be replaced again in a year. Brake rotors are the worse, then alternators, then water pumps, then....||||reg||1
Z||000008||dogtired||01-10-2000||02:30 AM||||Never took the wheel well out to change any but it is a thought??? Tightening the Alternator belt I always do through the wheel well?<BR>I have replaced 2 on V6s, both from the top.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> The 86 had straight forward mm botls. The 87 had torx head on most of them? That was a pain in the A$$.<BR>2birds said it! Getting the old gasket off seemed more of a pain than any thing.<BR>Just take your time and save $100.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Good luck||||reg||6
Z||000009||2birds||01-10-2000||02:31 AM||||terryk hit it right on the head.  I may have a lifetime replacement warranty on the $28 water pump, but it doesn't cover the time, permatex, anti-freeze, bacitracin, etc.  How is GM on OTC parts warranties, though, especially for high zoot wear items?<BR>I've found that a lot of parts stores may not stock the high quality aftermarket stuff (Raybestos, Bendix, Moog, IMHO), but they can order it. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||01-10-2000||01:43 PM||||GM's got some great warranties going. Many of their parts are lifetime or carry the labor warranty. If the part fails under the part warranty, they will replace it and do the labor for free even if you installed it.<P>I had the AC repaired by GM on my Dustbuster van. The warranty is lifetime on all parts and labor, forever. The compressor clutch died after 4 years and they replace it and serviced the system for free. <P>Not a bad deal. Of course a new alternator is $200, but then tend to last a long time.<P>I finally realized that if I planned on keeping the car for more than two years, the cost of GM parts is worth it. I still do the labor myself.<P>I have the Kent-Moore engine hanger they show in the manuals. It allows the engine and cradle to be lowered and it makes many repairs far easier. It could be easily fabricated from some channel iron or 2X4's. It would make access to the water pump a snap.<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000011||2birds||01-11-2000||10:47 PM||||I built one of those hangers when I changed my clutch, It worked, but with lateral clearance as tight as it is I would rather lower the engine/transaxle together in the cradle and use a cherry picker to hold the engine up to replace it next time.     ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Harry||01-11-2000||10:54 PM||||I did mine thru the top, took less time than I thought but insulation raws the arms up pretty good!||||reg||6
Z||000013||terryk||01-11-2000||10:56 PM||||2Birds:<P>With the long bolts, it can be hung pretty low (keep it clean guys). About a 2 foot drop.<P>But, you're right. It it pretty tight for some things.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Robert||01-14-2000||12:32 AM||||I thought I would let everyone who gave me advice know how my "dead" water pump turned out.  I decided to let the Fiero Shop ( <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.net/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroshop.net/</A>  )  replace it.  George recommended using a rebuilt pump, as all the new ones have plastic impellers. It took him 4 hours to do the job, including over an hour to chisel out two of the torx bolts that the last mechanic had stripped.  He also found that my distributor O-ring was leaking oil, so he changed it. By the way, he drove 55 miles out to my house with his trailer, to pick up my car.  I would recommend his shop to anyone in Northern California.<P>Robert<BR>||||reg||10
